 "equations": [],
 "operation": [
  {
   "topic": "Construction",
   "page": 1,
   "text": "The device is built from MOSFETs with a current-sensing circuit, works the same in both directions, and has low capacitance. It is meant to guard against short circuits, AC power cross, induced voltages and lightning surges. RoHS compliant, rated for Pb-free reflow, UL file E315805."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Tripping",
   "page": 3,
   "text": "In normal operation the part is a series resistance, Rdevice. When the line current goes above Itrigger the sensing circuit turns the MOSFETs off, and within tblock (about 1 µs) the current is held below Itrigger. If a voltage greater than Vreset stays across the part, the current then falls further to the quiescent level IQ, over a time that depends on the applied voltage."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Reset",
   "page": 3,
   "text": "After a surge the part returns to its conducting state once the voltage across it drops to Vreset. On a line with no DC bias, or a bias below Vreset (an unpowered signal line, for example), this happens on its own. On a line normally biased above Vreset the voltage across the part may never fall that low after the surge; such a line needs a deliberate way to bring it back, software monitoring being one."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Voltage limit",
   "page": 3,
   "text": "The voltage across the part must stay within its maximum rating. Where a fault could exceed it, an overvoltage protector limits it: a pair of MOVs, or a GDT on lines where bandwidth matters."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Temperature",
   "page": 3,
   "text": "Trip current falls as the junction warms: about 1.8 times its 25 °C value at -60 °C and about 0.7 times at 150 °C. Series resistance rises with temperature, from about 0.5 times its 25 °C value at -65 °C to about 2 times at 150 °C. Allowed power falls linearly to zero near 125 °C junction, from about 1.1 W at 20 °C with no extra copper and about 2.1 W with 0.5 sq. in. of added copper."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Pad layout and thermal copper",
   "page": 4,
   "text": "Terminations are 100 % matte tin. Solder-mask-defined pads should match the package pads in size and spacing; if smaller, they are centred on the terminals and at most 0.10-0.12 mm (0.004-0.005 in.) narrower or shorter, and never larger than the package pads. Stencil: 0.10-0.12 mm (0.004-0.005 in.) thick, apertures 0.025 mm (0.0010 in.) under the pad size. Copper extending beyond the solder pads lowers junction-to-ambient thermal resistance and junction temperature: from about 98 °C/W with none added to about 39 °C/W at 1 sq. in. and 35 °C/W at 2 sq. in."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Unused pad and parasitics",
   "page": 4,
   "text": "Every pad is soldered, NU and NC pads included, but those pads take no electrical connection. To keep parasitic capacitance low, no signal, ground or power trace runs under any pad."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Storage and assembly",
   "page": 2,
   "text": "Moisture sensitivity level 1; HBM ESD class 1B. Up to three reflow cycles per J-STD-020 with a 260 °C peak."
  }
 ],
